{"duration_ms":0,"id":18,"kind":"llm","model":"claude-opus-5","name":"claude-code.turn.11","provider":"anthropic","response":{"content":[{"text":"All 6 tests pass.\n\n**The bug** (`render.py:8`): the function escaped `<` and `>` but never `&`, so `Tom & Jerry` passed through with a raw ampersand.\n\n**The fix:** added `.replace(\"&\", \"&amp;\")` as the *first* replacement in the chain. Ordering is what makes this correct — the docstring already spelled out why, but the code didn't follow it. If `&` were escaped last, it would re-escape the ampersands that `&lt;`/`&gt;` just introduced and users would see `&amp;lt;`.\n\nI added three tests covering the ordering trap specifically:\n- `Tom & Jerry` → `Tom &amp; Jerry`\n- `<b>Tom & Jerry</b>` → `&lt;b&gt;Tom &amp; Jerry&lt;/b&gt;` (catches double-escaping if someone reorders the chain)\n- `&lt;` → `&amp;lt;` (a literal entity typed by a user is text, not markup)\n\nOne thing outside what you asked, worth flagging: the docstring claims this is safe for \"interpolation\" generally, but it doesn't escape `\"` or `'`. That's fine for text between tags, which is what a comment body is — but interpolating this output into an unquoted or single-quoted HTML attribute would still allow an attribute break. If comments are ever rendered into an attribute (a `title=` tooltip, say), that needs handling too. Python's `html.escape(text)` covers `&`, `<`, `>`, and `\"`/`'` with `quote=True` and would be the drop-in if you want that; I left the hand-rolled version in place since replacing it changes behavior beyond the reported bug.","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_011CevGuHHb8wTVGD1rEbVme","model":"claude-opus-5","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":138,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"cache_creation_input_tokens":138,"cache_read_input_tokens":18372,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"}},"start_ms":0,"usage":{"cache_read":18372,"cache_write_1h":138,"input":2,"output":1}}
